WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:02.530
Take a look at this scene

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:05.190
in 1961's "The Parent Trap."

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:06.756
It's pretty easy to spot

00:00:06.756 --> 00:00:08.490
where the shot is stitched together

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:12.070
to make it look like Hayley
Mills is playing two twins.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:15.940
Now take a look at this shot
from the 2019 movie "Us."

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:18.190
Lupita Nyong'o appears to be head-to-head

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:20.390
with her doppelgänger,
who's clutching her neck

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:22.400
as they grab each other by the wrists.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.673
To pull off this scene,
director Jordan Peele

00:00:24.673 --> 00:00:27.210
needed much more than a simple stitch.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.800
But creating complex
twin shots like this one

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.710
required a lot of building blocks

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.600
over more than 100 years of innovation.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.503
So, how did it happen?

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.843
Having one actor star in dual roles

00:00:39.843 --> 00:00:42.360
was a popular novelty
in the silent-film era

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.027
and in the early talkies.

00:00:44.027 --> 00:00:45.770
♪ Join the Navy band ♪

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:48.345
♪ When they play ♪

00:00:48.345 --> 00:00:50.240
See this shot from 1898,

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.620
where the illusionist
Georges Méliès created

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:55.520
four moving versions of
his head in one frame?

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.030
It was accomplished with
the use of matte shots,

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.930
which were kind of like
old-school green-screen composites

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.440
created by blocking
parts of the camera lens.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.030
It was with mattes that the
first split-screen effects

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:07.250
were pulled off.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:09.150
This is the traditional twin technique

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.500
you probably associate
with "The Parent Trap."

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:12.808
You shoot the scene twice,

00:01:12.808 --> 00:01:15.240
with the actor and a
stand-in switching places,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.860
then combine the two
strips of film into one.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.130
To disguise the seam,

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:22.010
filmmakers make use of
background elements in the shot,

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:23.210
like a doorframe.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.260
You can see that in "A Stolen Life,"

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.870
with two Bette Davises.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:29.328
Or here, where the
actor played twins again

00:01:29.328 --> 00:01:30.544
in "Dead Ringer."

00:01:30.544 --> 00:01:32.370
In these movies, you can also see the use

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.530
of the classic
over-the-shoulder technique,

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:37.840
where the actor speaks to a
stand-in filmed from behind.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.617
Split screens got complicated

00:01:39.617 --> 00:01:42.160
when one twin had to
interact with another.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.220
Take this scene from
"The Prisoner of Zenda,"

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:47.670
where Ronald Colman shakes
hands with his identical cousin.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.390
The filmmakers put glass
in front of the camera,

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:52.240
part of which they covered in masking tape

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.980
to matte out the head and
shoulders of the actor's double.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.610
After filming the scene,
they ran the film backwards

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:00.510
and filmed it again with
the actor on the other side,

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:01.560
this time matting out

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.618
everything but his head and shoulders.

00:02:03.618 --> 00:02:05.470
That's a lot of work for a handshake shot

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.480
that's only a few seconds long.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.680
The split screen also
usually required filming

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.450
with a locked and fixed camera,

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.020
resulting in shot compositions

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.610
that could look very designed,

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.670
with a sort of butterfly symmetry.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.378
So, how did you get from that

00:02:18.378 --> 00:02:19.936
to a shot like this,

00:02:19.936 --> 00:02:22.930
from David Cronenberg's
horror movie "Dead Ringers"?

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:24.700
In this film, not to be confused

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.220
with the earlier Bette
Davis movie "Dead Ringer,"

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:31.500
Jeremy Irons plays a disturbed
pair of twin gynecologists.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.060
The movie was a big deal at the time

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.470
because of how dynamic its shots were.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:36.750
In this scene, for example,

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.120
you see the twins walk and talk together

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.090
while followed by a fluid moving camera.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.150
And a shot like this was only possible

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.377
because of innovations from "Star Wars,"

00:02:46.377 --> 00:02:49.002
which, in 1977, was the first film

00:02:49.002 --> 00:02:51.680
to extensively use motion-control cameras.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.780
This was groundbreaking for twin effects

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:55.492
because directors could now program

00:02:55.492 --> 00:02:57.698
precise, repeatable camera movements

00:02:57.698 --> 00:03:00.515
to replicate the same
shot over and over again.

00:03:00.515 --> 00:03:03.220
This helped visual effects
artists film clean plates

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.770
with no actors in them
and use those plates

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:09.270
to help merge people into
the scene in postproduction.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:12.090
Motion control became a
mainstay of twin movies.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:15.080
And in 2002, it was among
several techniques used

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.820
to create the 130 twinning
shots in the movie "Adaptation,"

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:22.030
where Nic Cage plays the
identical Kaufman brothers.

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.530
One way the filmmakers
made the effects invisible

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.560
was with green-screen composites.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:28.800
That's when you film a
twin on a green screen,

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.290
isolate them, and comp them
into a shot with the other twin,

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:34.740
as seen here with the
two Seth Rogen characters

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.220
in "An American Pickle."

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.290
For this scene, Seth walked on a treadmill

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:39.380
in front of a green screen

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:41.450
so that when his two characters
were comped together,

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.720
they'd move at exactly the right pace.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.230
In "Adaptation," the
green-screen method only worked

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:48.660
for about 20% of the twin shots,

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.200
mostly in the scenes
that took place indoors.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.700
Outdoors, green screens can
create issues with green spill,

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.650
when that green light ends up
in places you don't want it.

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:00.240
In these cases, the
filmmakers on "Adaptation"

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.700
turned to rotoscoping,

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.190
which essentially means tracing by hand.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:06.890
With the rise of digital
rotoscoping in the '90s,

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:09.630
the technique became a
major player in twin movies,

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.650
allowing artists to comp together elements

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:13.680
of different shots even
if they weren't filmed

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.630
in front of a green screen.

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.380
It's one thing to make
two versions of an actor,

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:19.890
but what about 80?

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.777
That's how many versions of Hugo Weaving

00:04:21.777 --> 00:04:23.651
appear in the "Burly Brawl" sequence

00:04:23.651 --> 00:04:25.080
from "The Matrix Reloaded."

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.713
No amount of motion control,

00:04:26.713 --> 00:04:28.463
split screens, or rotoscoping

00:04:28.463 --> 00:04:31.700
could make a fight sequence
with this many clones possible.

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:34.670
Instead, this scene took
advantage of digital doubles.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:36.920
These CG humans were most often used

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.650
for wild stunt sequences

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:41.050
that no person could perform in real life.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:44.110
Because CG faces can
look rubbery up close,

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:46.830
digi-doubles are best used for wide shots,

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:48.680
like those of the Agent Smith clones

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:49.985
in "The Matrix Reloaded."

00:04:49.985 --> 00:04:53.320
CG humans are also super
expensive to create.

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:54.815
The "Burly Brawl" scene alone

00:04:54.815 --> 00:04:58.010
cost Warner Bros. an
estimated $40 million,

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:01.420
a big chunk of the film's
$150 million budget.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.410
So the digital-doubling method is best

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:04.790
for a single sequence,

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:06.990
not a workable option
for doubling an actor

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:08.900
throughout an entire movie.

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:10.860
Director David Fincher had to come up

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.990
with a different solution for
his film "The Social Network,"

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:16.600
where Armie Hammer played
the Winklevoss twins.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:18.753
Armie and his body double, Josh Pence,

00:05:18.753 --> 00:05:20.885
underwent a tailored training regimen

00:05:20.885 --> 00:05:23.670
to make their bodies
as similar as possible.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:26.530
To transpose Armie's
face onto Josh's body,

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:29.220
the filmmakers settled on
a facial-capture process

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:30.645
very similar to the one used

00:05:30.645 --> 00:05:33.061
in video games like NBA 2K.

00:05:33.061 --> 00:05:35.690
The VFX team first used
a medical-grade laser

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:38.590
to scan Josh's and Armie's
heads for digital models.

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:41.270
During filming, Josh wore
tracking markers on his face

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:43.730
so the VFX team could later object-track

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:45.040
his facial movements.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:48.180
After, they recorded Armie
inside a custom stage,

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.130
which had computer-controlled
lighting settings

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:52.230
to match the light in every scene.

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.650
In this stage, an array
of cameras recorded

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:56.690
every angle of Armie's face to provide

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:58.810
what is called a face texture map,

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:00.270
which could then be used to animate

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:01.780
the CG model of his head,

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:04.680
which, in turn, would get
mapped onto Josh's body.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.560
This basic process was also
used to clone Paul Rudd

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.910
for the Netflix series
"Living with Yourself,"

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:12.740
except Paul preferred to
act out his scenes alone,

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:15.970
reacting to his prerecorded
lines through an earpiece.

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:18.690
In scenes that required a
stand-in, like this one,

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.150
where Paul's character
resuscitates his clone,

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:23.900
the actor performed opposite
a guy in a green suit,

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:26.970
who VFX artists could replace later on.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.810
The elaborate face-scanning approach

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:30.330
wouldn't have been as feasible for "Us,"

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:32.060
which follows a family of four

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.067
confronting their evil doppelgängers.

00:06:34.067 --> 00:06:36.748
The film built on all the
innovations in twin effects

00:06:36.748 --> 00:06:38.960
over the past 100-plus years

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:41.480
to bring this nightmare scenario to life.

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:44.410
With the advancement of face,
head, and body replacements,

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:45.840
technology could now be used

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:48.030
in a Dr. Frankenstein kind of way

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:50.410
to harvest different limbs and body parts,

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:52.310
a similar method used to create the clones

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:54.300
in many scenes from "Orphan Black."

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:57.550
From the start, "Us" required
a lot of stunt doubles,

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:00.040
including a photo double and a body double

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:02.170
for each of the four main actors.

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:04.628
One producer joked that
there could be six versions

00:07:04.628 --> 00:07:06.890
of Winston Duke on set at a given time,

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.670
all dressed in Howard
sweatshirts and glasses.

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.290
Before shooting, Jordan and
the VFX team would figure out

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:14.650
which of the twins was guiding the action.

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:15.960
They'd capture that first

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:17.690
with the main actor playing the lead

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:19.460
and their double playing opposite.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:21.900
Then the actor and
double would swap places.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.260
After shooting, the VFX team
would patch the twin plates.

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:28.090
In many cases, they'd decide
to use a head from one plate

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:30.670
and track it onto the body of
the double in the other plate.

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:32.620
But often it got more complicated,

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:34.870
with artists piecing together by hand

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:37.900
various parts of an actor's
body from different shots.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:40.280
Most modern twin movies and TV shows

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:43.470
use motion-control cameras
to smooth out this process,

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:46.660
but in "Us," the script called
for lots of handheld shots

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:49.160
filmed with a more visceral camera style.

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:50.451
Without motion control,

00:07:50.451 --> 00:07:52.151
the crew had to take careful notes

00:07:52.151 --> 00:07:54.120
of all camera motion in each scene,

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:55.723
marking every position and tilt

00:07:55.723 --> 00:07:58.420
so they could replicate it
after swapping the actor.

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:00.690
There were also scenes that
the actors couldn't perform

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:01.600
with a real double,

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:03.850
like this face-off in the hall of mirrors,

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:06.210
when the young version of
Lupita Nyong'o's character

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:08.980
gets choked by her evil doppelgänger.

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:10.555
The actor, Madison Curry,

00:08:10.555 --> 00:08:12.650
needed something to really grab onto,

00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:15.610
so they had her clutch a
paper cup wrapped in green,

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:17.873
which would be digitally
replaced later on.

00:08:17.873 --> 00:08:19.870
A similar technique was used in "Enemy,"

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:21.652
where Jake Gyllenhaal acted opposite

00:08:21.652 --> 00:08:23.243
a tennis ball on a stick,

00:08:23.243 --> 00:08:25.476
its height representing
his double's eyeline.

00:08:25.476 --> 00:08:28.120
This method can make the
VFX team's job easier,

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:31.090
because it means there isn't a
whole body to erase later on,

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:32.648
as seen here for "Orphan Black"

00:08:32.648 --> 00:08:34.640
and "An American Pickle."

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:36.930
None of these innovations
happened in a vacuum.

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:39.810
Even in 2019, "Us" relied
on some of Hollywood's

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:42.930
tried and true methods, like
the good old split screen,

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:44.330
for a number of shots.

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:46.717
Every once in a while on
set, Jordan Peele would say,

00:08:46.717 --> 00:08:49.300
"I'm thinking this shot
is a Hayley Mills."

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:51.600
The dynamic twin scenes in his movie

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.370
were a culmination of more than 12 decades

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:56.361
of Hollywood ingenuity.

